=== Part 1: the typed same-run accepted-counterfactual authority ===
OK   MINT ON ACCEPT: the single ACCEPT observation carries a typed authority, trusted only by `isAcceptedCounterfactual`
OK   the authority is a detached, deeply frozen, SCALAR-only snapshot (no Date, Map or Set), holding the exact same-run basis and baseline-placements outcomes
OK   the snapshot content: P at 10:30, O displaced and relocated 10:00 -> 15:00, complete counterfactual rows (baseline O replaced in place, P last)
OK   NO AUTHORITY WITHOUT ACCEPT: a REJECT / unavailable observation has no typed authority
OK   FORGERY REJECTED: an object literal with the same fields is not an accepted counterfactual
OK   FORGERY REJECTED: a Object.create(authority) delegate is not an accepted counterfactual
OK   FORGERY REJECTED: a JSON round trip is not an accepted counterfactual
OK   FORGERY REJECTED: a frozen copy is not an accepted counterfactual
OK   FORGERY REJECTED: null is not an accepted counterfactual
OK   FORGERY REJECTED: a string is not an accepted counterfactual
OK   FORGERY REJECTED: an accepted flag object is not an accepted counterfactual
OK   mintAcceptedCounterfactual over the real authorities: the predicate ACCEPTs and the authority exists
OK   a predicate REJECT mints NOTHING (the brand is applied only when P4b3 itself returns ACCEPT)
OK   a throwing predicate propagates exactly as before (the P4b4 composition isolates it per pair) and mints nothing
OK   garbage input: P4b3 answers UNAVAILABLE / EVALUATION_FAILED, nothing is minted, nothing throws
OK   an unrepresentable (non-finite) instant after an ACCEPT yields the acceptance WITHOUT an authority (consumers treat it as not accepted)
OK   LINK RULES: an authority links only to an ACCEPT observation of the SAME candidate, and only a minted authority links
OK   a legitimate link resolves to that exact authority
OK   BASELINE BINDING: the authority is bound to the exact baseline result object it was minted with, and only that object
OK   NOT A BASELINE: a non-object / null / undefined / forged authority never matches, and the question never throws
OK   A MINT WITHOUT A BASELINE OBJECT MINTS NOTHING: the ACCEPT stands but no authority exists (fail closed)
OK   IN-PLACE MODIFICATION: the genuine bound object, modified AFTER the mint, is no longer the run's baseline (the fingerprint taken at the mint differs)
=== Part 2: the P4c1 V1 selector ===
OK   ONE ACCEPT -> APPLY with that candidate, its typed authority and selectionReason LAST_KNOWN_OPPORTUNITY_RESCUED
OK   ZERO ACCEPT (REJECT only) -> NO_CHANGE / NO_ACCEPT
OK   READY with no observations -> NO_CHANGE / NO_ACCEPT
OK   RUN-LEVEL failure / not READY (UNAVAILABLE run) -> NO_CHANGE / INCOMPLETE_OBSERVATION
OK   every run-level unavailable reason is INCOMPLETE_OBSERVATION
OK   a hostile / malformed run never throws and fails closed (INCOMPLETE_OBSERVATION)
OK   an ACCEPT observation WITHOUT its typed authority (a copy of a real one) -> INCOMPLETE_OBSERVATION (never APPLY)
OK   a real ACCEPT plus an authority-less ACCEPT could hide a second accept -> INCOMPLETE_OBSERVATION
OK   MULTIPLE ACCEPTS (real run with 2 ACCEPTs): NO_CHANGE / MULTIPLE_ACCEPTS -- no ranking, no winner
OK   ORDER-INDEPENDENT: all 2 permutations of the observation set give ONE decision
OK   one ACCEPT among REJECTs: every permutation APPLYs the same candidate
OK   DETERMINISTIC: repeated selection is byte-identical (same authority object)
OK   NO SCORE, NO CLIENT FACTS: the selection is {status, reason | candidateIntentId, acceptedCounterfactual, selectionReason} and nothing else
OK   an ORDINARY generation unavailable (NO_ACTIONABLE_PROMOTION_SLOT) is not a technical failure: the other single ACCEPT still APPLYs
OK   an ORDINARY acceptance unavailable (INCONSISTENT_AUTHORITY from P4b3) is not a technical failure either
OK   GENERATION_FAILED on one observation -> INCOMPLETE_OBSERVATION even though P1 is the only ACCEPT (the failure could hide a second ACCEPT)
OK   EVALUATION_FAILED on one observation -> INCOMPLETE_OBSERVATION (fail closed)
OK   and the clean run (both promotions observed) is MULTIPLE_ACCEPTS, not APPLY
